    "text": "96\n\n The #1 Closing Mistake That Could Wreck Your Deal (And How to Avoid It)\n\nHi Sara,\nLet\u2019s talk about a sneaky little deal-killer: closing mechanics.\nI know, I know\u2014sounds like something only lawyers and escrow agents should care about. But if you\u2019re buying or selling an accounting firm (or any business, really), this is the one thing you cannot afford to mess up.\nSee, your agreements are written based on a plan for closing. If everyone (buyer, seller, and lawyers) isn\u2019t on the same page about what that looks like, things can go sideways\u2014fast.\nTwo Ways to Close a Deal (And Why It Matters)\nMost people assume that when you sign a purchase agreement, boom! The deal is done. That\u2019s the old-school, \u201csimultaneous sign and close\u201d approach. No difference between the signing date, closing date, and effective date\u2014it\u2019s all one and the same. Simple, right?\nExcept when it\u2019s not.\nBanks and state agencies love to complicate things. Okay okay, so do lawyers!\u00a0\nBut get a load of this chicken and egg situation:\u00a0\nMost lenders won\u2019t schedule your closing until they see a final approved (and sometimes) signed purchase agreement.\u00a0\nSometimes the lender won\u2019t close until you get a state agency\u2019s sign off, but in order to get that sign off, the state requires a signed agreement. So somehow you need to sign the purchase agreement BEFORE you can actually confirm you have the money to purchase the business.\u00a0\nSo if you\u2019re relying on financing, you\u2019d better believe you need an escape hatch in case the bank pulls the plug at the last second (which certainly \u00a0happened in 2020).\nThat\u2019s why we almost always structure deals as a delayed sign-and-close. The idea?\nYou sign the purchase agreement as soon as both parties are ready.\n\nYou close at a later date, once financing, tax clearances, and other conditions are met.\n\nThere are built-in escape clauses (a.k.a. \u201cconditions precedent\u201d) so nobody gets stuck in a deal they can\u2019t afford to close.\n\nWhat Actually Happens on Closing Day?\nSo if you\u2019ve already signed the purchase agreement, what\u2019s left to do at closing? Glad you asked.\nOn closing day, you\u2019ll finalize:\n\u2714 The Wire Transfer \u2013 Because sellers don\u2019t work on the honor system.\n\u2714 The Promissory Note \u2013 A legally binding \u201cI.O.U.\u201d that shouldn\u2019t be signed until we are really sure things are going the distance.\n\u2714 The Bill of Sale \u2013 Officially transfers ownership.\n\u2714 Assignment & Assumption Agreements \u2013 Ensures the buyer is stepping into the seller\u2019s legal shoes.\n\u2714 The Closing Certificate \u2013 A final \u201ctruth check\u201d that confirms everything the seller promised originally is still true on closing day.\nMess this up, and you could be in for a world of legal and financial headaches.\nThe Bottom Line\nIf you\u2019re buying or selling an accounting practice, get clear on your closing mechanics early.\u00a0\nA sloppy approach can lead to unnecessary risk, frustrated buyers and sellers, and even lawsuits.\nTo smooth deals and stress-free closings,\nSara Sharp\nPartner, SK&S Law Group\nBusiness Consultant and Founder of Deal Academy\nP.S. If you're looking to buy or sell an accounting firm, and want me to keep an eye out for you, fill out this form here.\n\n For more helpful lessons from Deal Academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down, where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out some recent Dealmakers podcast episodes by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2025\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
